### What is fault tolerance?

Fault tolerance in distributed systems means two things: durability, so committed data is never lost, and availability, so the service keeps responding even when components fail. Kafka delivers both through replication, and the replication factor is the foundation.

```java
package com.bank.ft;

public class FaultToleranceDefinition {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Fault Tolerance = Durability + Availability");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Durability: committed data never lost");
        System.out.println("  -> Replication + acks=all + min.insync.replicas");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Availability: service keeps responding");
        System.out.println("  -> ISR-based leader election");
        System.out.println("  -> Automatic failover to surviving replicas");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Fault Tolerance = Durability + Availability
Durability: replication + acks + min.insync.replicas
Availability: ISR-based leader election
```

### How does the replication factor provide redundancy?

With a replication factor of three, every partition exists as three copies on three brokers. Losing one or even two brokers still leaves a copy. If you had a replication factor of one, a single broker failure would lose that partition permanently.

```java
package com.bank.ft;

public class RedundancyThroughReplication {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Replication Factor and Redundancy:");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("RF=1: [Broker1]         -> 1 failure = DATA LOST");
        System.out.println("RF=2: [Broker1, Broker2] -> 1 failure = safe");
        System.out.println("RF=3: [B1, B2, B3]       -> 2 failures = safe");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Banking: RF=3 on all financial topics");
        System.out.println("Rajesh's transfer survives two broker crashes");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
RF=1: 1 failure = DATA LOST
RF=2: 1 failure = safe
RF=3: 2 failures = safe (banking standard)
```

### How does ISR-based leader election provide availability?

When the leader's broker fails, the controller promotes a new leader from the ISR. Because every ISR member has all committed records, the new leader has the full committed history. Failover preserves data and resumes service quickly.

```java
package com.bank.ft;

public class LeaderElectionFailover {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Leader Election Failover:");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Before: ISR = {Broker1(L), Broker2, Broker3}");
        System.out.println("Broker 1 crashes at 10:00:00");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Controller detects failure");
        System.out.println("Elects Broker 2 as new leader (ISR member)");
        System.out.println("Broker 2 has offsets 0..1500 (all committed)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("After: ISR = {Broker2(L), Broker3}");
        System.out.println("Service resumed at 10:00:02 (2 seconds)");
        System.out.println("No committed records lost");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Rajesh's transfers: all safe, processing continues");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Broker 1 crashes -> Broker 2 elected -> 2 second failover
No committed records lost. Service continues.
```

### How do acks and min.insync.replicas provide durability?

The `acks` setting controls when a producer considers a write successful. With `acks=all`, the leader acknowledges only after every ISR member has stored the record. `min.insync.replicas` sets the floor: if the ISR shrinks below this number, the leader rejects writes.

```java
package com.bank.ft;

public class AcksAndMinIsr {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("acks and min.insync.replicas:");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("acks=0:   fire and forget (may lose data)");
        System.out.println("acks=1:   leader only (leader crash = lost)");
        System.out.println("acks=all: all ISR members (strongest durability)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("min.insync.replicas=2 with acks=all:");
        System.out.println("  ISR={B1,B2,B3}: write -> B1,B2,B3 store -> ACK");
        System.out.println("  ISR={B1,B2}:    write -> B1,B2 store -> ACK");
        System.out.println("  ISR={B1}:       write -> REJECTED (ISR < min)");
        System.out.println("  NotEnoughReplicasException thrown");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
acks=all + min.insync.replicas=2:
  3 ISR: write confirmed on 3 brokers
  2 ISR: write confirmed on 2 brokers
  1 ISR: write REJECTED (safety first)
```

### How do all three mechanisms work together?

These three mechanisms must be configured together to actually get fault tolerance. Replication factor three alone is not enough if the producer uses `acks=1`, because a record acknowledged by only the leader can vanish if the leader dies before followers copy it.

```java
package com.bank.ft;

public class ThreeMechanismsTogether {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("All Three Mechanisms Together:");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("1. Replication Factor = 3");
        System.out.println("   -> 3 copies on 3 brokers (redundancy)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("2. ISR + Leader Election");
        System.out.println("   -> New leader from caught-up replicas (availability)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("3. acks=all + min.insync.replicas=2");
        System.out.println("   -> Write confirmed on >= 2 brokers (durability)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Together: survive 1 broker failure with no data loss");
        System.out.println("          and continued write availability");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Replication (redundancy) + ISR election (availability) + acks=all (durability)
= fault tolerance for banking
```

### What are common mistakes with fault tolerance configuration?

The most common mistake is using `acks=1` with replication factor three, thinking the data is safe because there are three copies. With `acks=1`, the leader acknowledges after storing locally, and a leader crash before followers copy the record loses it.

```java
package com.bank.mistakes;

// WRONG: RF=3 but acks=1. Leader crash loses uncommitted records.
public class WrongAcksSetting {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("WRONG: RF=3, acks=1");
        System.out.println("  Leader stores record -> ACK sent to producer");
        System.out.println("  Leader crashes BEFORE followers copy it");
        System.out.println("  New leader elected -> record NOT there");
        System.out.println("  Result: Rajesh's transfer LOST");
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.mistakes;

// CORRECT: RF=3, acks=all, min.insync.replicas=2
public class CorrectFaultTolerantConfig {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("CORRECT: RF=3, acks=all, min.insync.replicas=2");
        System.out.println("  Leader stores record");
        System.out.println("  Followers store record");
        System.out.println("  ACK sent to producer (on 2+ brokers)");
        System.out.println("  Leader crashes -> new leader has the record");
        System.out.println("  Result: Rajesh's transfer SAFE");
    }
}
```

```text
WRONG: RF=3 + acks=1 = false sense of security, data can be lost
CORRECT: RF=3 + acks=all + min.insync=2 = true fault tolerance
```

### What failure scenarios should you plan for?

With the banking recipe (RF=3, min.insync=2, acks=all), one broker failure is fully tolerated: writes continue and no data is lost. If two brokers fail, the ISR drops below min.insync.replicas, so the leader stops accepting writes to protect durability. Reads may still work if one replica survives.

```java
package com.bank.ft;

public class FailureScenarios {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Failure Scenarios (RF=3, min.insync=2, acks=all):");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("0 broker failures: normal operation");
        System.out.println("  ISR=3, writes OK, reads OK");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("1 broker failure:");
        System.out.println("  ISR=2 (>= min.insync=2), writes OK, reads OK");
        System.out.println("  Leader elected from ISR, no data loss");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("2 broker failures:");
        System.out.println("  ISR=1 (< min.insync=2), writes REJECTED");
        System.out.println("  Reads may work if surviving replica is leader");
        System.out.println("  Safety: no data loss, but service degraded");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("3 broker failures:");
        System.out.println("  No replica available, partition OFFLINE");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
0 failures: normal
1 failure: writes + reads OK, no data loss
2 failures: writes rejected, reads maybe OK
3 failures: partition offline
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Fault tolerance is not a single switch but a balance of durability, availability, and latency. For money movement you always choose durability, even at the cost of rejecting writes when too many brokers are down. Monitor under-replicated partitions and ISR-shrink alerts as early warnings that your safety margin is eroding.

```java
package com.bank.senior;

public class FaultToleranceSeniorChecklist {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Senior Fault Tolerance Checklist:");
        System.out.println("1. RF=3, min.insync=2, acks=all (banking recipe)");
        System.out.println("2. unclean.leader.election.enable=false");
        System.out.println("3. Rack-aware replica placement across zones");
        System.out.println("4. Monitor: under-replicated partitions");
        System.out.println("5. Monitor: ISR-shrink events");
        System.out.println("6. Alert on min.insync.replicas threshold breach");
        System.out.println("7. Plan for zone-level failures, not just broker");
        System.out.println("8. Test failover regularly (chaos engineering)");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Senior checklist: banking recipe, rack-awareness,
monitoring ISR-shrink and under-replicated partitions,
testing failover regularly.
```
